Enhanced SID Player (.mus player)
---------------------------------
The `sidplay` task (`src/libs/sidplay.inc`) is a port of the
Enhanced SID Player player routine.

Original: "Enhanced SID Player" by Craig Chamberlain (1986), published
in "COMPUTE!'s Music System for the C64 and C128" (COMPUTE!
Publications).

Disassembly: "ComputeSidPlayerC64Source" by Chris Zinn
(October-November 2025):
https://github.com/MyDeveloperThoughts/ComputeSidPlayerC64Source

Neither the 1986 original nor the 2025 disassembly carries an explicit
license. This component is included as a user-loadable music player;
redistribution is at the user's own discretion with respect to this
component.

Compute's Gazette Sid Collection (CGSC) tunes -- gorkanoid
---------------------------------------------------------
`music/katmand.mus`, `music/immig.mus` and `music/firstdt.mus` are tunes from
the Compute's Gazette Sid Collection (CGSC) -- the archive of songs written for
Compute's Gazette's Sidplayer, which is the same music system as the Enhanced
SID Player this repository ports (see the entry above). They are played as
published: a `.mus` file is already a Sidplayer score, so nothing is converted.

Source: https://www.c64music.co.uk/
Mirror: archive.org, "Compute's Gazette Sid Collection"

The `grknoid` task plays them one per mode -- katmand in the attract loop,
immig during a game, firstdt once on the game-over screen -- and credits the
collection on its attract screen.

Tetris "A Theme (Korobeiniki)" - sheet music source
---------------------------------------------------
The melody and bass line of `music/tetris.mus` were read from a MIDI made
from a piano sheet. It is credited here exactly as the sheet prints it:

  "Tetris - A Theme (Korobeiniki)"
  NinSheetMusic sheet 574, https://www.NinSheetMusic.org/
  Traditional Russian Folk Song
  Arrangement by Hirokazu Tanaka
  Piano Arrangement by Olimar12345
  Nintendo (C) 1989

The MELODY is a traditional Russian folk song and is in the public domain.
The sheet, the piano arrangement and Nintendo's 1989 game arrangement are
not, and none of them carries a licence that permits redistribution of
derivative works. This repository uses the notes to produce its own
three-voice SID score -- the chords, the harmony, the waveforms, the
envelopes and the A A B form are this repository's own work, and
`tools/gen-mus.py` regenerates the tune from that MIDI -- and it does NOT
redistribute the sheet, its PDF or the MIDI: none of the three is in this
repository, so rebuilding the tune needs the MIDI from the user. The credit
above is given because the notes came from that arrangement; redistribution
of `music/tetris.mus` is otherwise at the user's own discretion with respect
to it. For anything beyond personal use, ask the arranger via NinSheetMusic.

Tetris (tetris.c64)
-------------------
The `gortris` task is a port of the game logic from Wiebo de Wit's
"Tetris for the CBM64 in 6502 Assembly" (`reference/tetris/`).

None of the original's artwork is used: its screens and charset are
char-mode CharPad data, and gortris draws its own field, frame, panel and
text with the kernel's glyph support. Its music is not used either — the
reference embeds a SID tune and plays it from its own raster IRQ, while
gortris plays `music/tetris.mus`, this repository's own three-voice SID
arrangement of the **Korobeiniki** melody -- a traditional Russian folk song,
in the public domain. Its melody and bass line were read from a MIDI made from a
fan piano sheet, credited in the sheet-music entry above; the chords, the
harmony, the waveforms, the envelopes and the form are this repository's own
work. The tune was written, not converted: a `.sid` is a program with
its own player and IRQ, so it cannot be turned into a Sidplayer `.mus` score.
`tools/gen-mus.py` generates it, and it is played through the shared sound
library (`sid.lib`, see the entry above), so the chip stays under the
operating system's control.

